**[Question 1] - What is the problem?**

Can a schema-aware, uncertainty-calibrated relational learner outperform both feature-flattening tabular models and graph neural networks, and remain reliable when a real multi-table database undergoes temporal, entity, relationship, and heterophilous structural distribution shifts?

**[Question 2] - Why is it interesting and important?**

Most predictive ML studies either flatten a database into one table or begin with a preconstructed graph. Both choices can hide the contribution of relational structure: flattening loses variable-length neighborhoods and relationship paths, while a fixed graph benchmark hides the decisions involved in extracting entities, keys, timestamps, and heterogeneous attributes from native tables. A shift-aware answer would establish when relational inductive bias actually helps, rather than assuming that a more complicated GNN is better.

This would give the community a reproducible decision framework for choosing between tabular, graph, and hybrid methods. It would also make benchmark scores more meaningful for deployment, where future entities, new relationships, changing populations, and missing or delayed tables are routine. Reliable uncertainty estimates could support abstention, human review, and risk-sensitive applications such as recommendations, fraud detection, and customer or content prediction. The resulting raw-table benchmark and standardized shift protocol would enable future work on relational representation learning, robust GNNs, and database-aware foundation models to be compared on the same evidence.

**[Question 3] - Why is it hard?**

The learner must combine heterogeneous tables with different schemas, cardinalities, and data types without leaking labels or future information through joins. A single entity can have a variable-size, time-dependent neighborhood, and important evidence may lie along several relationship paths; naïve flattening creates duplicated rows and arbitrary aggregation, while naïve message passing can oversmooth, overcount repeated events, or scale poorly. Heterophilous links may connect entities with dissimilar labels, so neighbor averaging can actively damage predictions.

The evaluation is difficult as well. Random row splits overestimate performance when entities or relationships recur across train and test. Temporal splits can confound concept drift with missing history; entity-disjoint and relationship-disjoint splits test different inductive abilities; and structural shifts change degree, schema usage, or label informativeness. Models must therefore be compared with leakage-safe splits, equal preprocessing budgets, and uncertainty metrics, not only a single accuracy number. Calibration is especially hard under shift: confidence can remain high even when the representation is out of distribution. Finally, billion-scale tables require sampling and indexing strategies whose approximation error must be measured rather than silently ignored.

**[Question 4] - Why hasn't it been solved before?**

Earlier work has developed strong but separated branches. Tabular systems and relational feature engineering preserve convenient table semantics but usually materialize selected joins and aggregates, limiting long-range and dynamic relational effects. GNN and graph-learning benchmarks provide controlled graph inputs, but the graph and features have already been constructed, so they do not test raw multi-table extraction, schema choices, or join-induced leakage. Existing relational benchmarks improve coverage of multiple tables and tasks, yet head-to-head studies across these branches have generally emphasized IID performance and do not jointly test inductive, transductive, temporal, and structural shifts with calibrated uncertainty.

The missing ingredient is a common protocol that preserves native tables, reconstructs graphs transparently, and evaluates robustness across several shift axes. Privacy, licensing, evolving schemas, class imbalance, and the cost of rebuilding time-respecting neighborhoods have also discouraged such studies. This proposal is explicitly a robustness-and-calibration extension to existing relational benchmarks, rather than a claim that prior benchmark coverage is absent. It addresses the gap with public, versioned datasets; released extraction and split code; database-level provenance checks; and a model family whose relational aggregation, tabular encoding, and uncertainty head can be ablated independently. Its claim is therefore testable even if the hybrid model does not win everywhere: it can identify the conditions under which each inductive bias is appropriate.

**[Question 5] - What are the key components of my approach and results?**

1. **Dataset and protocol.** Build a leakage-audited suite from public RelBench-style relational datasets and additional public multi-table sources where licensing permits. Retain native tables, primary/foreign keys, timestamps, entity types, missingness, and relationship multiplicities. Define entity-attribute and relationship-prediction tasks in both transductive and inductive settings, with the available history and feature visibility specified separately for each. For every task release (i) a chronological future split, (ii) leakage-safe entity-disjoint and relationship-disjoint splits, and (iii) severity-controlled structural shifts that vary degree, relation mixture, attribute missingness, and label informativeness. Define relation-specific heterophily using neighbor-label agreement (and label informativeness relative to a degree-matched null), rather than assuming one global homophily value. Keep a standard IID split only as a reference.

2. **Proposed method.** Use a schema-aware relational encoder with typed table/column embeddings, type-specific projections, and time-decayed event representations. Sample bounded, provenance-tracked neighborhoods and aggregate along multiple key-defined paths with relation-specific attention or gated message passing. Fuse the resulting entity representation with a strong tabular encoder (CatBoost/LightGBM-style features or a differentiable GBDT-compatible head). Add an ensemble or evidential uncertainty head trained with proper scoring objectives, plus an OOD score combining representation distance and predictive disagreement. The system should support inductive entities by computing representations from their available rows and transductive entities by optionally caching historical states.

3. **Baselines and ablations.** Compare (a) majority/frequency and target-encoding baselines, (b) leakage-safe SQL aggregation plus LightGBM/CatBoost, (c) row-wise MLPs, (d) standard GCN, GraphSAGE, and GAT, (e) heterophily-oriented H2GCN, MixHop, GPR-GNN, and LINKX, (f) relational message-passing models, (g) graph-only GNNs built from the same extracted relations, and (h) the proposed hybrid. For uncertainty, compare uncalibrated predictions with temperature scaling, isotonic regression, deep/seed ensembles, and the proposed uncertainty head. Ablate relation paths, temporal decay, typed parameters, neighbor sampling, graph versus tabular fusion, calibration training, and each uncertainty signal. Report preprocessing and inference cost, memory, sampled-neighbor count, and performance by entity degree, heterophily, and missingness.

4. **Metrics and analysis.** Use ROC-AUC and PR-AUC for imbalanced binary tasks, macro-F1 or balanced accuracy for multiclass tasks, and MAE/RMSE for regression. Report mean and 95% confidence intervals over seeds and datasets, paired per-task improvements, and worst-group performance. Separate (i) predictive performance, (ii) calibration—negative log-likelihood, Brier score, expected and adaptive calibration error, and reliability diagrams—from (iii) OOD detection—AUROC/AUPR and false-positive risk at fixed coverage—and (iv) selective prediction—risk/coverage curves and risk at fixed coverage. For regression, report interval coverage and width. Evaluate robustness by relative degradation from IID to each severity-controlled shift and worst-shift score. Include runtime, peak memory, and scaling curves.

5. **Expected results.** These are hypotheses to test, not assumed outcomes: relational models should help most when labels depend on multi-hop or variable-length neighborhoods; tabular aggregation may remain competitive on local homogeneous tasks; and the hybrid may provide the best average accuracy–cost trade-off. Typed/gated aggregation may reduce failures on heterophilous relations and temporal encodings may reduce future-split degradation. The main expected result is a statistically supported map from data properties and shift severity to the best model choice, with paired per-task and worst-shift analyses. A calibrated hybrid would be successful only if it reduces selective risk and improves OOD detection without unacceptable calibration or computational cost.
